// Fresh Question Bank - Domain 3.0 Security Architecture
window.FRESH_BANK = (window.FRESH_BANK || []).concat([
// ---------- 3.1 Architecture models (30) ----------
{o:"3.1",q:"A company runs its workloads on IaaS virtual machines. Which task falls to the customer rather than the provider?",c:["Patching the hypervisor hosts that run the virtual machines","Replacing failed storage hardware in the provider data center","Patching the guest operating system on each virtual machine","Patching the database engine inside a fully managed database service"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. The hypervisor layer is operated by the provider in every cloud model, and the customer cannot even reach it. It would be the customer's job only in a private cloud the customer builds itself.","Incorrect. Physical hardware, including disks, is always the provider's responsibility in public cloud. A customer only handles hardware in on-premises or colocation designs where it owns the equipment.","Correct. In IaaS the provider secures hardware and virtualization, but everything above the hypervisor, including the guest OS, belongs to the customer. The customer must patch, harden and monitor each VM it deploys.","Incorrect. Patching the engine of a managed database is the provider's job because that is a PaaS offering. It would be the customer's task only if the database were installed by hand on an IaaS VM."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A company subscribes to a SaaS customer relationship management (CRM) application. Which task still remains with the customer?",c:["Defining user roles and access permissions in the tenant","Applying security patches to the servers that host the CRM","Maintaining physical access controls at the hosting facility","Updating the hypervisors underneath the CRM workloads"],a:0,e:["Correct. Even in SaaS, the customer owns its identities, access configuration and the data it enters. The provider cannot decide who in the customer's organization should see which records.","Incorrect. The SaaS provider runs and patches the application and its servers. Patching would be the customer's task only in IaaS, or in on-premises software that the customer installs itself.","Incorrect. Physical security of data centers is handled by the provider in every cloud model. The customer would handle it only for equipment housed in its own facility or cage.","Incorrect. Hypervisors are part of the provider's infrastructure and are invisible to SaaS customers. A customer manages virtualization only in a private cloud or on-premises environment it operates."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A company runs workloads in its own data center and in Azure, and the workloads must exchange data securely. Which factor is MOST essential for this hybrid design to work seamlessly?",c:["Replicating all on-premises data to a second public cloud region as a backup","Placing a CASB in front of the on-premises servers to broker user sessions","Moving the on-premises workloads onto an isolated, air-gapped segment","Secure, dependable links such as a site-to-site VPN or a dedicated circuit"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Cross-region replication improves resilience, but it does not let the on-premises and Azure workloads communicate. It would be the right answer if the stem asked about disaster recovery.","Incorrect. A CASB mediates access between users and cloud services and does not carry server-to-server traffic. It would fit a question about enforcing policy on SaaS usage by employees.","Incorrect. Air-gapping removes network connectivity, which is the opposite of what these workloads need. It suits classified or safety-critical systems that must never connect to other networks.","Correct. A hybrid cloud only functions if the two environments can talk over a secure, dependable link, with identity and management integrated across them. VPN or private circuits provide that path."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"Developers upload individual functions that run in response to events and are billed per execution. They never size, provision or patch any instances. Which model is this?",c:["Platform as a Service with reserved application instances","Serverless computing (Function as a Service, FaaS)","Infrastructure as a Service with auto scaling groups","Container orchestration on self-managed Kubernetes nodes"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. PaaS also hides the operating system, but applications run on provisioned instances that are billed while reserved. It would fit if the team deployed a long-running app rather than event-driven functions.","Correct. Serverless/FaaS abstracts all servers: the provider runs and scales the code on demand and charges per invocation. The customer only supplies the function and its configuration.","Incorrect. With IaaS the customer still defines, patches and scales virtual machines, even when auto scaling adds them. It would fit if the team managed server images.","Incorrect. Self-managed Kubernetes requires the team to operate the nodes and the control plane. It would fit a team that wants portability and is willing to run its own cluster infrastructure."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A team defines its VPCs, subnets and firewall rules in Terraform templates kept in Git and deployed by a pipeline. Which is a key SECURITY benefit of this approach?",c:["Code review of the templates replaces the need for runtime configuration monitoring","Peer-reviewed templates produce repeatable builds that reduce drift and manual misconfiguration","Templates stored in Git allow the pipeline to run with broad admin rights because changes are logged","Redeploying from templates keeps every guest OS patched without further maintenance"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Review catches mistakes before deployment, but resources can still be changed by hand afterward. Runtime monitoring and drift detection remain necessary alongside reviewed templates.","Correct. Infrastructure as code makes environments version-controlled, reviewable and identical on every deployment, which cuts the manual errors and drift that cause misconfigurations.","Incorrect. Logging does not justify excessive privileges. Pipelines still need least-privilege credentials, because a compromised pipeline with admin rights could rebuild the whole environment maliciously.","Incorrect. Redeployment uses whatever image the template references, so stale images stay stale. Patching requires updating base images or running patch management, not merely rerunning the template."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A shared Terraform module is reused to build dozens of environments. Which risk is created BY that reuse of a single template?",c:["Configuration drift when administrators edit deployed resources by hand in the cloud console","Shadow IT from teams buying cloud services outside procurement channels, bypassing IT review","VM sprawl from forgotten instances that are never decommissioned or patched by their owners","A secret or insecure setting in the module is copied into every environment built from it"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Drift is a real IaC concern, but it comes from manual changes after deployment, not from reusing a template. It would be the answer if the stem described out-of-band console edits.","Incorrect. Shadow IT is about unapproved services acquired outside IT's control. It would be the answer if departments were using unsanctioned SaaS or cloud accounts instead of the shared module.","Incorrect. Sprawl is caused by unmanaged lifecycle, with instances left running and unpatched. It would fit a scenario where resources are created and abandoned rather than copied from one template.","Correct. A single flaw or embedded credential in a reusable template spreads to every deployment automatically. Scan templates, keep secrets in a vault, and review module changes before release."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"An application is split into 40 small services that call each other over APIs. Which security implication is MOST significant?",c:["Many more endpoints and calls, each needing authentication, authorization and monitoring","Looser change control, because patching one service forces redeployment of the whole application","A single point of failure at the central application server that hosts all business logic","Reduced need for API security because the traffic stays inside the cluster network"],a:0,e:["Correct. Microservices multiply the number of interfaces and trust relationships, enlarging the attack surface. Each call needs mutual authentication, authorization and logging, often via a service mesh and an API gateway.","Incorrect. This describes a monolith, where one patch redeploys everything. Microservices let teams update individual services independently, so this is not the implication here.","Incorrect. A central application server hosting all logic is a monolithic design. Microservices spread functions out, which usually lessens single points of failure while adding complexity.","Incorrect. Internal traffic still needs protection, since an attacker who gets one foothold can move laterally. This assumption is the opposite of zero trust practice."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A network team wants a central controller to program forwarding rules on switches and routers through an API, separating the control plane from the data plane. Which technology provides this?",c:["Network function virtualization (NFV)","Virtual LAN (VLAN) segmentation","Software-defined networking (SDN)","Virtual private cloud (VPC) peering"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. NFV runs functions such as firewalls and routers as software on standard servers. It virtualizes the appliances but does not itself split control and data planes; it would fit a question about replacing hardware appliances.","Incorrect. VLANs segment a layer 2 network into separate broadcast domains. They would be the answer if the goal were to isolate departments on shared switches, not to centralize control.","Correct. SDN centralizes control-plane decisions in a controller that pushes policy to data-plane devices, enabling automation and consistent security policy across the network.","Incorrect. VPC peering links two cloud virtual networks so they can exchange traffic. It would fit a question about connecting separate cloud networks, not centralizing control of forwarding."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A platform team wants to run many isolated applications on one host without a separate guest OS per application, accepting that a kernel flaw could affect all of them. Which technology fits?",c:["Type 1 hypervisor with a guest OS per VM","Type 2 hypervisor hosting desktop VMs","Dedicated bare-metal servers per application","Containerization with a container runtime"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A type 1 hypervisor gives each VM its own kernel and guest OS, so a kernel flaw stays inside one VM. It would be best when strong isolation matters more than density.","Incorrect. Type 2 hypervisors also give every VM its own guest OS, running on top of a host OS. They suit desktop and lab use rather than sharing one kernel across applications.","Incorrect. Dedicated servers isolate by hardware, with one workload per machine, and share nothing. They would be right where regulatory or performance needs justify the cost.","Correct. Containers share the host kernel and isolate applications at the process level, so they are light and fast. The shared kernel is also why a kernel vulnerability can affect every container."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"An engineer compares virtual machines and containers. Which statement is accurate?",c:["Each container runs its own guest OS, whereas VMs share the host operating system kernel","Each VM runs its own guest OS, whereas containers share the host operating system's kernel","VMs depend on a container runtime for isolation, whereas containers require a hypervisor","VMs expose a shared kernel that one flaw can compromise, whereas containers are isolated by hardware"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. This reverses the relationship. A container has no guest kernel of its own, and it is VMs that carry a complete operating system each.","Correct. VMs virtualize hardware and each carries a full OS and kernel, while containers virtualize at the OS level and share the host kernel. That is why containers are lighter but isolate less strongly.","Incorrect. This mixes up the technologies: VMs run on hypervisors, while containers run on a container runtime such as containerd. Neither depends on the other's isolation layer.","Incorrect. This also reverses the model. VMs are separated by the hypervisor and have their own kernels, while containers are the ones sharing a kernel and relying on OS-level isolation."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A classified research network has no wired or wireless link to any other network. Data enters only on approved, scanned removable media. Which design is this?",c:["Logically segmented VLAN with ACLs","Screened subnet behind two firewalls","Physically isolated air-gapped network","Isolated VPC without an internet gateway"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A VLAN with ACLs separates traffic logically but shares physical infrastructure that can be misconfigured. It fits segmentation of departments, not a network with no connections at all.","Incorrect. A screened subnet hosts public-facing services between firewalls, so it is connected by design. It would fit a web server that must be reachable from the internet.","Correct. An air gap is complete physical separation from other networks. The trade-off is that removable media becomes the main attack path, hence the scanning procedure.","Incorrect. A VPC without an internet gateway is isolated from the internet but still runs on shared provider infrastructure and can be connected by VPN or peering. It is not a physical air gap."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A company assigns HR, Finance and Guest users to separate VLANs, all carried by the same physical switches. Which segmentation approach is this?",c:["Logical segmentation using VLANs on shared hardware","Physical segmentation using dedicated switches per department","Air gap with removable media transfers","Hybrid cloud connectivity over a VPN"],a:0,e:["Correct. VLANs separate traffic into distinct broadcast domains in software while the same switches carry it, which is logical segmentation. Inter-VLAN routing policy then controls what may cross.","Incorrect. Physical segmentation would use separate switches and cabling for each group. It offers stronger isolation but costs more and would fit very high-security separations.","Incorrect. An air gap means no network connection at all, so data moves only on media. These departments stay connected through routed VLANs.","Incorrect. Hybrid cloud links an on-premises environment with a cloud provider. It has nothing to do with dividing a local network into broadcast domains."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A factory uses controllers that open valves and run motors based on sensor input, all supervised from a central human-machine interface (HMI). Which term BEST describes the supervisory environment?",c:["ICS / SCADA supervisory control network","Embedded controller firmware platform","Real-time operating system (RTOS)","Internet of Things (IoT) deployment"],a:0,e:["Correct. ICS/SCADA covers the supervisory and control systems that monitor and run physical industrial processes through PLCs, sensors and HMIs.","Incorrect. Embedded systems are individual purpose-built devices, such as a PLC, that sit inside the larger ICS. They describe a component, not the supervised environment.","Incorrect. An RTOS is the operating system type that guarantees time-bounded responses on many controllers. It describes software on a device and not the supervisory environment as a whole.","Incorrect. IoT refers to networked consumer or commercial smart devices. It would fit connected sensors reporting to a cloud platform instead of a supervised industrial process."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"Which challenge is MOST characteristic of ICS/SCADA environments?",c:["Rapid release cycles that outpace the security team's ability to test and approve every update","Shared kernel exposure between tenants on common virtualization hosts, where one flaw reaches all","Decades-long lifecycles, scarce patch windows and unauthenticated legacy protocols like Modbus","Dependence on a third-party provider for physical security and power of the hosting facility"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Fast release cycles describe DevOps and web application development. ICS changes slowly, and updates are rare precisely because they risk disrupting production.","Incorrect. Shared-kernel tenancy is a container concern. Most ICS devices run dedicated controllers or RTOSes and are not co-hosted with other tenants.","Correct. ICS equipment runs for decades, uptime comes first, and protocols like Modbus have no built-in authentication or encryption. Segmentation and monitoring compensate for the lack of patching.","Incorrect. Reliance on a provider's physical security is a cloud issue. ICS is usually on-premises, so the organization controls the plant's physical access itself."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A smart thermostat runs a tiny operating system on a microcontroller with very little memory and no practical way to push updates. Which term BEST describes it?",c:["SCADA server supervising industrial controllers","Embedded IoT device with constrained on-board resources","Mainframe terminal connected to a central host","Thin client workstation using a remote desktop"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A SCADA server supervises industrial controllers and is a full computer. It would fit a plant control room rather than a small consumer device.","Correct. Embedded and IoT devices are purpose-built with constrained resources and often weak or absent update mechanisms, which makes them hard to secure.","Incorrect. A mainframe terminal accesses a large central computer. It does not describe a standalone device with its own microcontroller and firmware.","Incorrect. A thin client is a lightweight endpoint that relies on a server for processing. A thermostat runs its own firmware and is not a remote display for server-hosted desktops."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"An automotive braking controller must respond within strict, guaranteed time limits. Which type of operating system does it MOST likely use?",c:["General-purpose desktop OS with priority scheduling","Hardened container host OS","Thin-client operating system","Real-time operating system (RTOS)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Desktop operating systems cannot guarantee worst-case timing, even with priority tuning, because background services and drivers can delay tasks. They suit general computing.","Incorrect. A hardened container host reduces attack surface for server workloads but still offers no hard timing guarantees. It fits cloud-native deployments instead of safety controllers.","Incorrect. A thin-client OS is designed to display remote sessions with minimal local processing. It cannot provide the deterministic control loop that braking needs.","Correct. An RTOS provides deterministic, time-bounded responses, which is required when a late result could cause a safety failure."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"After a storage failure, a review measures how many staff-hours are needed to bring an application back into service. Which architecture consideration does this measurement address?",c:["Scalability for demand growth","Responsiveness under normal load","Ease of recovery after failures","Ease of deployment of new builds"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Scalability is about adding or removing capacity as demand changes. It would be the answer if the review asked whether the design could handle growth.","Incorrect. Responsiveness describes how quickly the system answers requests under normal operation. It would fit performance tests rather than restoration effort.","Correct. Ease of recovery covers how simply and quickly an architecture can be restored after failure, and staff-hours to restore is a direct measure of it.","Incorrect. Ease of deployment concerns releasing new builds or systems. It differs from restoring service after something failed."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"An architecture review asks whether the design can handle ten times today's users next year by adding capacity as demand grows. Which consideration is being evaluated?",c:["Scalability of the system design","Availability of the service","Resilience to component faults","Ease of recovery after outages"],a:0,e:["Correct. Scalability is the ability to grow or shrink capacity with demand, whether by scaling up or scaling out.","Incorrect. Availability measures how much of the time a service is up. It would be the answer if the question were about uptime targets.","Incorrect. Resilience is the ability to keep working despite faults or attacks. It would fit a question about surviving failures, not accommodating growth.","Incorrect. Ease of recovery covers restoring service after an outage. Growth in users is a capacity issue, not a restoration issue."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A payment API must keep processing transactions even if one data center loses power. Which consideration is being prioritized?",c:["Scalability through elastic autoscaling","Responsiveness through low-latency design","Ease of recovery through documented restore procedures","Resilience through redundancy and failover"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Autoscaling adds capacity under load, but it does not keep the service running when a whole site loses power. It would fit a question about traffic spikes.","Incorrect. Low latency makes responses fast, but it does nothing to keep the service up during an outage. It would fit performance requirements.","Incorrect. Documented restore procedures speed recovery after an outage, but the requirement is to avoid interruption in the first place. Recovery planning supports the stricter resilience goal.","Correct. Resilience means continuing to operate through component or site failures, which is achieved with redundancy and automatic failover across data centers."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"Which set of systems is MOST affected by the architecture consideration of an 'inability to patch'?",c:["Multi-tenant SaaS platforms that the vendor updates centrally","Embedded devices, ICS controllers and legacy hosts","Container images rebuilt from patched base images","Serverless functions on provider-managed runtimes"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. The SaaS vendor patches its own platform, and customers have nothing to apply. Inability to patch is not the central concern there.","Correct. Embedded, ICS and legacy systems often cannot be patched because of vendor limits, uptime needs or end of life, so compensating controls such as segmentation are needed.","Incorrect. Containers are immutable, so patching means rebuilding from an updated base image and redeploying. That is routine, not an inability.","Incorrect. The provider maintains the serverless runtime, and the customer updates only its own function code and dependencies."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A company deploys to an in-country cloud region even though it costs more and adds latency for most users. Which consideration drove this choice?",c:["Elastic scalability to absorb large seasonal demand spikes","Compliance with data residency and sovereignty laws","Responsiveness for users located near the region","Cost optimization through lower regional pricing"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Scalability concerns handling growth in load, and it would not justify a more expensive region that adds latency. The stem gives no capacity driver.","Correct. Laws and regulators that require records to stay within a country or region can outweigh cost and performance, so the region is chosen for compliance.","Incorrect. The stem says latency is worse for most users, so responsiveness cannot be the reason. It would apply if a nearby region had been chosen for speed.","Incorrect. The in-country region costs more, so cost cannot be the driver. Cost optimization would favor the cheapest region regardless of location."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A server team installs a hypervisor directly on host hardware, with no general-purpose OS underneath, to reduce attack surface for production VMs. Which hypervisor type is this?",c:["Type 2 (hosted) hypervisor","Container runtime with shared kernel","Application virtualization layer","Type 1 (bare-metal) hypervisor"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A type 2 hypervisor, such as VirtualBox or VMware Workstation, runs as an application on a host OS. It suits labs and desktops rather than production hosts.","Incorrect. A container runtime shares the host OS kernel instead of virtualizing hardware, so it is not a hypervisor. It would fit lightweight application isolation.","Incorrect. Application virtualization streams or isolates individual applications on a desktop OS. It does not host whole virtual machines.","Correct. A type 1 hypervisor such as ESXi, Hyper-V or KVM runs directly on hardware, which is more efficient and exposes less attack surface than running on top of a full OS."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A company moves a monolithic application unchanged onto cloud virtual machines instead of re-architecting it. What is the security implication?",c:["The application keeps its existing flaws, and the customer still patches the OS and runtime","The provider now patches the application layer because the workload runs on its infrastructure","The workload becomes serverless and inherits the provider's managed runtime security controls","The application becomes isolated from the internet by default inside the provider network"],a:0,e:["Correct. Lift and shift carries existing flaws into the cloud, and with IaaS the customer remains responsible for the OS, runtime and application security.","Incorrect. Provider responsibility ends at the infrastructure layer in IaaS. The application remains the customer's concern wherever the hardware sits.","Incorrect. Moving to VMs does not change the architecture into functions. Serverless requires refactoring the code to run on a managed runtime.","Incorrect. Cloud networks are not isolated automatically, and a lifted workload keeps its exposure unless security groups and routing are configured to limit it."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A cloud contract includes a document that assigns each security control to either the provider or the customer for the service model purchased. What is this document called?",c:["Service level agreement (SLA)","Memorandum of understanding (MOU)","Cloud shared responsibility matrix","Master service agreement (MSA)"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. An SLA defines measurable service targets such as uptime and the credits owed if they are missed. It does not assign control ownership.","Incorrect. An MOU records a general intent to cooperate and is usually non-binding. It would fit an early-stage agreement between organizations.","Correct. A responsibility matrix spells out, per service model, who handles each security task, preventing gaps in patching, logging and configuration.","Incorrect. An MSA sets the overall commercial and legal terms between parties. It may reference the matrix but is not the document that maps controls to responsibilities."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A startup runs identical workloads on AWS and Google Cloud. Which statement BEST explains the security benefit of this multi-cloud design?",c:["A single consistent IAM model applies across both providers' native tooling","Staff need expertise in only one provider's tools and settings","Security baselines are shared automatically between the two platforms","A flaw or outage at one provider does not take down every workload"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Each provider has its own IAM model and policy language. Consistency requires a third-party tool or extra effort.","Incorrect. Multi-cloud requires skills in each platform's tooling, consoles and APIs, so the staffing burden grows rather than shrinks. A single-provider design is what would reduce the expertise needed.","Incorrect. Baselines are not shared automatically. Each provider needs its own configuration, or a cloud security posture tool that normalizes them.","Correct. Using more than one provider adds platform diversity and resilience, avoiding dependence on a single vendor, though it increases complexity."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"Which statement correctly compares centralized and decentralized architectures from a security view?",c:["Decentralized is easier to govern but concentrates risk; centralized spreads risk but is harder to secure","Centralized is easier to govern but concentrates risk; decentralized spreads risk but is harder to secure","Centralized is harder to monitor because logs are scattered; decentralized offers one point of enforcement","Both designs share the same risk profile because identical controls apply in each one"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. This reverses the trade-off. Spreading components across many locations complicates governance, not simplifies it.","Correct. Centralization simplifies policy, monitoring and control but creates a single point of failure and attractive target. Decentralization limits blast radius but makes uniform security harder.","Incorrect. Scattered logs and multiple enforcement points are characteristics of decentralization, not of a centralized design.","Incorrect. The designs do not share a risk profile. Concentration of control and distribution of components create different failure modes and administrative burdens."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A hospital buys networked infusion pumps that run vendor-controlled firmware. Which approach is BEST for securing them?",c:["Install endpoint antivirus on each pump and join them to the domain patch cycle","Allow direct internet access so vendor updates arrive as soon as they are released","Place them on a dedicated VLAN with strict ACLs and monitor traffic for anomalies","Join the pumps to the corporate Wi-Fi under the standard laptop security baseline"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Medical devices rarely allow third-party agents or domain membership, and unapproved changes can void support or affect safety. Antivirus fits general-purpose endpoints.","Incorrect. Direct internet exposure widens the attack surface. Vendor updates should arrive through a controlled path after validation.","Correct. Constrained, vendor-controlled devices cannot run agents or be patched by the hospital, so network segmentation, traffic restriction and monitoring are the practical controls.","Incorrect. A laptop baseline cannot be applied to a device with fixed firmware, and placing it on the general corporate network exposes it to lateral movement."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A developer asks why containers in the company's Kubernetes cluster should not run as root. What is the BEST reason?",c:["A container escape from a root process yields root control of the shared host kernel","Root-owned containers bypass Kubernetes network policies and can reach every pod directly","Root processes cannot bind to the ports that Kubernetes services require to run","Root-owned containers cannot be scheduled across multiple nodes in the cluster"],a:0,e:["Correct. Containers share the host kernel, so a compromise combined with root privileges makes escape and host takeover more damaging. Least privilege applies to containers, too.","Incorrect. Network policies apply to pods regardless of the user inside the container. Segmentation is a separate control from process privilege.","Incorrect. Running as root can bind privileged ports, which is not the concern. Non-root containers typically use higher ports, with services mapping to them.","Incorrect. Scheduling is unrelated to the user a process runs as. Kubernetes can place root and non-root containers across nodes alike."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A web tier must stay available if an entire availability zone fails. Which design BEST meets this requirement?",c:["Redundant instances behind a load balancer in several availability zones","One larger instance with more CPU and memory in a single availability zone","Nightly snapshots of a single instance copied to another region for recovery","An auto scaling group restricted to one availability zone with health checks"],a:0,e:["Correct. Spreading redundant instances across zones removes the single point of failure, and the load balancer routes around a failed zone with no downtime.","Incorrect. A larger instance improves capacity but remains a single point of failure inside one zone. It would suit a performance bottleneck.","Incorrect. Cross-region snapshots support recovery, but restoring takes time, so the service is down meanwhile. They fit RPO goals rather than availability.","Incorrect. Auto scaling replaces failed instances, but if they all sit in one zone, a zone failure removes them together. It would be right only with multiple zones."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A company uses a hosted cloud environment that is dedicated to its organization alone, with no other tenants. Which deployment model is this?",c:["Public cloud deployment model","Community cloud deployment model","Private cloud deployment model","Hybrid cloud deployment model"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A public cloud is multi-tenant infrastructure shared by many customers. It would fit an environment where tenants share hosts.","Incorrect. A community cloud is shared by several organizations with common requirements, such as agencies or hospitals. This environment has a single tenant.","Correct. A private cloud is dedicated to a single organization, whether it is hosted on-premises or by a provider.","Incorrect. A hybrid cloud combines two or more models, such as private and public, with connectivity between them. The stem describes only one environment."]},

// ---------- 3.2 Secure enterprise infrastructure (32) ----------
{o:"3.2",q:"A company places its public web and mail servers in a zone that is separated from both the internet and the internal LAN by firewalls. Which zone is this?",c:["Extranet segment for partner organizations","Screened subnet (DMZ) between two firewalls","Intranet segment for internal users","Out-of-band management network for administrators"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. An extranet is a controlled segment that gives specific partners access to selected resources. It would fit a design for supplier portals rather than public-facing servers.","Correct. A screened subnet hosts internet-facing services between firewalls, so a compromise of those servers does not directly expose the internal LAN.","Incorrect. The intranet is the trusted internal network used by employees and is not exposed to the internet. Public servers placed there would give attackers a path inward.","Incorrect. A management network carries administrative traffic separately from production traffic and should never be internet reachable. It would fit device administration, not public services."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A security appliance must detect AND block malicious traffic in real time rather than only generate alerts. Which device and deployment satisfy this?",c:["Network IDS fed by a TAP on the core switch","NetFlow collector receiving router exports","Honeypot placed beside the production servers","Network IPS deployed inline in the traffic path"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. An IDS on a TAP sees copies of traffic and can only alert. It is best when the priority is zero impact on the network, not prevention.","Incorrect. NetFlow records metadata about conversations for analysis and anomaly detection. It does not inspect payloads or block anything.","Incorrect. A honeypot is a decoy for detecting and studying attackers. It does not filter production traffic.","Correct. Only a device sitting inline can drop or reset malicious packets as they pass, which is the role of an intrusion prevention system."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"An analyst needs a complete copy of network traffic for monitoring, and even a sensor failure must not interrupt production traffic. Which option is BEST?",c:["An inline IPS between the core and edge switches","A forward proxy with TLS inspection enabled","A passive network TAP feeding a monitoring sensor","A reverse proxy in front of the servers"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. An inline IPS sits in the path, so a failure or misconfiguration can disrupt traffic unless it fails open. It is the right choice when blocking is required.","Incorrect. A forward proxy is an inline intermediary for user web requests. It would break connectivity if it failed and does not provide a full copy of network traffic.","Correct. A TAP passively duplicates traffic to a sensor, so if the sensor fails, production traffic keeps flowing unaffected.","Incorrect. A reverse proxy brokers inbound requests to servers and is also in the path. It does not capture all traffic for analysis."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A single inline firewall with no redundant peer fails. Which failure mode keeps traffic flowing, prioritizing availability over security?",c:["Fail-open mode applied to the device","Fail-closed mode on the device","Implicit deny rule at the end of the ACL","Active/passive failover to a standby unit"],a:0,e:["Correct. Fail-open lets traffic pass when the device fails, preserving availability at the cost of leaving traffic uninspected.","Incorrect. Fail-closed blocks all traffic on failure, protecting security but causing an outage. It is chosen when confidentiality matters more than uptime.","Incorrect. Implicit deny is the default rule that rejects traffic no rule allows. It is part of rule processing and not a device failure behavior.","Incorrect. Active/passive failover needs a second device to take over. The stem states there is no redundant peer, so this option cannot apply."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A bank decides that if its inline IPS crashes, all traffic must stop rather than pass uninspected. Which mode is this?",c:["Fail-open mode on the device","Active/active clustering of two devices","Passive monitor mode on a TAP","Fail-closed mode on the device"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Fail-open allows traffic through on failure to keep services running. It would be right if downtime were costlier than temporarily losing inspection.","Incorrect. Active/active clustering is a redundancy design in which multiple devices share load. It does not define what happens when the whole function fails.","Incorrect. Monitor mode only observes and alerts, so traffic is never blocked. It fits tuning or detection-only deployments.","Correct. Fail-closed prioritizes security over availability, blocking traffic whenever the control cannot inspect it. Banks and regulated environments often choose this."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A company wants a firewall that can allow Microsoft Teams while blocking BitTorrent, even though both use common ports, for outbound user traffic. Which device is BEST?",c:["Layer 4 stateful inspection firewall","Layer 7 next-generation firewall (NGFW)","Stateless packet-filtering router ACL","Web application firewall (WAF) cluster"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A layer 4 stateful firewall sees only addresses, ports and connection state, so two applications on the same port look identical. It is efficient for simple port-based policies.","Correct. A next-generation firewall identifies applications by inspecting content, regardless of port, so it can apply per-application policy to user traffic.","Incorrect. A router ACL filters on header fields at layers 3 and 4 and has no connection awareness. It is lightweight but cannot identify applications.","Incorrect. A WAF protects a web application from inbound HTTP attacks. It does not classify or control the applications that users run outbound."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A public web application must be protected against SQL injection and cross-site scripting in HTTPS requests, with malicious requests blocked. Which device is MOST appropriate?",c:["Layer 4 stateful firewall","Web application firewall (WAF)","Network access control (NAC) appliance","Network IDS on a SPAN port"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A layer 4 firewall cannot see inside the HTTP payload, so the injection string looks like normal allowed traffic. It is right for port and protocol filtering.","Correct. A WAF inspects HTTP/S requests at the application layer, recognizes injection and XSS patterns, and can block them.","Incorrect. NAC controls which devices may join the network. It would fit endpoint admission, not request-level attacks on a web app.","Incorrect. A SPAN-fed IDS may detect signs of the attack but is passive and cannot block. It would be better where alerting only is acceptable."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A small business wants firewall, IPS, antivirus, content filtering and VPN functions in a single appliance. Which device is this?",c:["Secure web gateway (SWG) proxy","Load balancer with SSL offloading","Web application firewall (WAF)","Unified threat management (UTM)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. An SWG focuses on filtering web traffic, with URL filtering and malware scanning. It does not provide IPS or VPN termination as a bundle.","Incorrect. A load balancer distributes traffic across servers and may offload TLS. It is not a security suite.","Incorrect. A WAF protects web applications from HTTP-layer attacks only. It would fit a hosting scenario, not a multifunction perimeter device.","Correct. A UTM combines several security functions in one device, which is convenient for small environments but creates a single point of failure."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Administrators must reach servers in a secure zone only through a single hardened, monitored host. Which device provides this?",c:["Jump server (bastion host)","Forward proxy for outbound web traffic","Reverse proxy for inbound web traffic","Honeypot decoy for attackers"],a:0,e:["Correct. A jump server is the single controlled entry point for administration, which concentrates auditing and hardening in one place.","Incorrect. A forward proxy relays users' outbound web requests and does not provide controlled administrative access to a zone.","Incorrect. A reverse proxy shields servers by handling inbound client requests. It is not an administrative gateway.","Incorrect. A honeypot is a decoy designed to attract attackers. Administrators do not use it to reach production servers."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Employees' web requests pass through a server that caches content, filters URLs and hides internal client addresses from the internet. What is it?",c:["Reverse proxy for server requests","Load balancer for a server pool","Forward proxy for user requests","Jump server for administrators"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A reverse proxy sits in front of servers and handles inbound requests from the internet. It would be the answer if the stem protected a web server.","Incorrect. A load balancer spreads incoming requests across a pool of servers. It does not filter or cache users' outbound browsing.","Correct. A forward proxy acts on behalf of internal clients going out, enabling caching, filtering and address masking.","Incorrect. A jump server provides administrative access into a secure zone and does not broker user web traffic."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A server sits in front of the company's web servers, terminates TLS, caches static content and forwards filtered requests, so clients never see the real server addresses. What is it?",c:["Forward proxy for client requests","Jump server for administrators","Honeypot decoy for attackers","Reverse proxy for the web tier"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A forward proxy serves internal clients going out to the internet. The stem describes traffic coming in to servers.","Incorrect. A jump server gives administrators a controlled path into a secure network and does not serve client requests.","Incorrect. A honeypot is bait for attackers and does not forward legitimate client traffic to real servers.","Correct. A reverse proxy handles inbound requests for the servers behind it and often provides TLS offload, caching and filtering while hiding backend addresses."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Which device distributes incoming requests across a pool of web servers and removes failed servers from rotation to improve availability?",c:["Network TAP feeding a sensor","Application delivery load balancer","Core router between subnets","Network IDS sensor on a mirror port"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A TAP copies traffic to a monitoring tool. It does not make forwarding decisions or balance load.","Correct. A load balancer spreads traffic across servers and uses health checks to stop sending requests to unhealthy ones.","Incorrect. A router forwards packets between networks based on destination addresses and does not perform health checks on application servers.","Incorrect. A network IDS analyzes traffic for attack signs and generates alerts. It does not distribute requests."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Before a laptop can join the wired network, the switch verifies its certificate and confirms it has current antivirus signatures, otherwise placing it in a quarantine VLAN. Which technology is this?",c:["Port security with sticky MAC addresses","Split-tunnel VPN client configuration","Network access control (NAC) with 802.1X","Port mirroring to a monitoring system"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Port security limits which MAC addresses can use a port but does not check certificates or antivirus status. It is a lighter control against rogue devices.","Incorrect. Split tunneling is a VPN routing choice for remote users and has no role in network admission.","Correct. NAC authenticates the device, commonly with 802.1X, and checks its posture before granting access or isolating it.","Incorrect. Port mirroring copies traffic to a monitoring tool for analysis and cannot enforce admission or quarantine anything. It would fit a question about feeding an IDS sensor."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"In an 802.1X deployment, the access switch relays EAP messages between the laptop and the RADIUS server and enforces the authorization result. Which role does the switch play?",c:["Authenticator (port enforcement point)","Supplicant (client requesting access)","Authentication server (credential decision)","Policy administration point (policy authoring)"],a:0,e:["Correct. The authenticator, typically a switch or wireless access point, passes EAP traffic between client and server and opens or closes the port based on the result.","Incorrect. The supplicant is the client device requesting access, which is the laptop in this scenario. The switch is the intermediary that relays its EAP messages.","Incorrect. The authentication server, usually RADIUS, validates the credentials and makes the decision. The switch merely relays the exchange and enforces the result.","Incorrect. A policy administration point is an access-control concept used in authorization architectures, where policies are authored. It is not part of the 802.1X exchange."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A company needs a framework that can carry certificates, tokens or passwords for both wired and wireless 802.1X authentication. Which should it use?",c:["Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP)","Wi-Fi Protected Access 3 (WPA3)","Internet Protocol Security (IPsec)","Challenge-Handshake Authentication Protocol (CHAP)"],a:0,e:["Correct. EAP is an extensible framework that carries many authentication methods, such as EAP-TLS and PEAP, over 802.1X on wired and wireless networks.","Incorrect. WPA3 secures wireless links and its enterprise mode actually uses EAP underneath. It does not itself provide a flexible method framework for wired ports.","Incorrect. IPsec protects IP traffic with encryption and authentication, typically in VPNs. It does not authenticate devices at the switch port.","Incorrect. CHAP is a single challenge-response password method, mainly for PPP links. It is not an extensible framework."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A switch is configured so only the first learned MAC address on each port can send traffic, and any other device causes the port to shut down. Which feature is this?",c:["BPDU guard on access ports","DHCP snooping on untrusted ports","Port security on access ports","Dynamic ARP inspection (DAI) on VLANs"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. BPDU guard shuts down an edge port that receives spanning tree BPDUs, which blocks rogue switches. It does not check source MAC addresses.","Incorrect. DHCP snooping classifies ports as trusted or untrusted to stop rogue DHCP servers. It validates DHCP messages, not source MACs.","Correct. Port security restricts a port to specific MAC addresses and can disable it on a violation.","Incorrect. Dynamic ARP inspection validates ARP packets against the snooping table to stop ARP spoofing. It is not port-level MAC limiting."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A company needs to permanently connect its headquarters and branch networks over the internet, with gateways encrypting traffic between the sites. Which VPN type is this?",c:["Remote-access VPN with a client","Site-to-site IPsec VPN using tunnel mode","Clientless TLS VPN browser portal","HTTPS-protected intranet website"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A remote-access VPN connects individual users' devices to the network and requires client configuration. It fits traveling staff.","Correct. A site-to-site VPN links whole networks, usually with IPsec between gateways, so users need no client software.","Incorrect. A clientless TLS portal gives an individual user browser access to selected applications. It does not join two networks.","Incorrect. HTTPS secures traffic for a single web application between a browser and a server. It does not join two networks, which is what a site-to-site tunnel does."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Contractors need encrypted access to internal web applications from unmanaged laptops using only a browser, with no client software installed. Which solution is BEST?",c:["IPsec site-to-site VPN between gateways","L2TP/IPsec remote-access VPN with client","SSH tunnel through a bastion host","Clientless TLS VPN portal via browser"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Site-to-site IPsec links whole networks through gateways, so it requires VPN equipment on both ends. It does not give individual contractors browser-based access from unmanaged laptops.","Incorrect. L2TP/IPsec remote access requires VPN client configuration on each device, which conflicts with the no-software requirement.","Incorrect. An SSH tunnel needs an SSH client and technical setup. It suits administrators more than contractors using browsers.","Correct. A clientless TLS VPN publishes internal applications through the browser over HTTPS, so nothing needs installing on unmanaged devices."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"An IPsec VPN encapsulates the entire original packet, including its IP header, inside a new packet for transit between two gateways. Which mode is this?",c:["Transport mode ESP configuration","Authentication Header (AH) protocol","Tunnel mode ESP configuration","IKE Phase 1 key negotiation"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Transport mode protects only the payload and keeps the original header, so it is used between two hosts directly.","Incorrect. AH is a protocol that provides integrity and authentication. It can run in either mode, so it does not describe the encapsulation.","Correct. Tunnel mode wraps the whole original packet with a new header, which is why it is used for gateway-to-gateway VPNs.","Incorrect. IKE Phase 1 negotiates the security association and keys. It sets up the tunnel but is not a packet encapsulation mode."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Which IPsec protocol provides confidentiality through encryption in addition to integrity and authentication?",c:["Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP)","Authentication Header (AH)","Internet Key Exchange (IKE)","Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE) tunnel"],a:0,e:["Correct. ESP encrypts the payload and can also authenticate it, providing confidentiality, integrity and origin authentication.","Incorrect. AH authenticates packets and protects integrity, but it does not encrypt. Use it only where confidentiality is not required.","Incorrect. IKE negotiates security associations and keys for IPsec. It does not protect data packets itself.","Incorrect. GRE is a tunneling protocol that carries other protocols, and it offers no encryption on its own. It is often paired with IPsec."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A branch office wants to use several internet links, managed centrally, with policy-based path selection for each application. It does not need cloud-delivered security services. Which technology fits?",c:["Secure Access Service Edge (SASE)","MPLS provider private circuit","Site-to-site IPsec tunnels with static routes","Software-defined WAN (SD-WAN) overlay"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. SASE adds cloud-delivered security services, such as SWG and ZTNA, on top of SD-WAN. The stem explicitly excludes those services.","Incorrect. An MPLS circuit is a provider-managed private WAN link. It would be one underlay among the links but does not offer policy-driven path selection over internet connections.","Incorrect. Static routes with IPsec give fixed paths and lack dynamic, application-aware selection. They are simpler but less flexible.","Correct. SD-WAN uses software and centralized policy to steer traffic over multiple WAN links based on application and link quality."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A company wants cloud-delivered security services, including SWG, CASB, ZTNA and firewalling, combined with SD-WAN for users wherever they work. Which architecture is this?",c:["Unified threat management (UTM) at each branch","Secure Access Service Edge (SASE)","Dual-firewall screened subnet","Network access control with 802.1X"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. UTM bundles security functions in an on-premises appliance. It does not deliver cloud-based services to roaming users.","Correct. SASE converges networking and security as a cloud service delivered close to users, regardless of location.","Incorrect. A dual-firewall screened subnet is a network zone design at one site. It does not cover remote users or cloud services.","Incorrect. NAC controls admission of devices to a local network, and has no cloud security services or WAN optimization."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Where should a network IDS sensor be placed to see traffic that has actually passed the perimeter firewall, without the noise of internet scans?",c:["Directly on the internet-facing side of the perimeter firewall, before any filtering","Immediately behind the perimeter firewall, on the internal or DMZ segment","On each user laptop as a host-based agent installed by endpoint management","On the ISP's upstream router, upstream of the company's own equipment"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A sensor in front of the firewall sees all hostile traffic, including what the firewall already blocks. It can be useful for threat intelligence but creates noise.","Correct. A sensor behind the firewall sees only traffic that was allowed in, which reduces false positives and focuses on real threats.","Incorrect. A host agent is a HIDS, which monitors a single endpoint and not network traffic crossing a segment.","Incorrect. The organization does not control the ISP's equipment, and traffic there is even noisier than outside the firewall."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A security architect wants to reduce the attack surface of the company's servers. Which action does this BEST?",c:["Increase logging verbosity on every internet-facing service","Add a second WAF in front of every exposed service","Deploy a honeypot beside the production servers","Disable unused services and close unneeded ports"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. More logging improves detection and forensics, but exposure stays the same. It is a detective control.","Incorrect. An additional WAF adds protection to services that remain exposed, so the surface is unchanged. It is a compensating layer.","Incorrect. A honeypot is a deception tool that lures attackers. It adds an exposed system instead of reducing exposure.","Correct. Removing services, ports and interfaces that are not needed leaves fewer entry points to attack, which is the definition of reducing the surface."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A firewall's rule base contains: 1) allow any to web server on 443, 2) deny all. An administrator appends a rule 3 to block one malicious IP to the web server. What happens?",c:["The malicious IP is still allowed because rule 1 matches first","The malicious IP is blocked because specific rules take precedence regardless of position","The malicious IP is blocked because explicit denies are evaluated before any allow rules","The firewall rejects the new rule as conflicting with the existing rules"],a:0,e:["Correct. Firewalls process rules top-down and stop at the first match, so the broad allow in rule 1 permits the attacker. The block rule must be placed above it.","Incorrect. Most firewalls do not rank rules by specificity. Position decides, which is why the order of rules is critical.","Incorrect. Deny rules are not evaluated before allows unless they are placed first. The first matching rule wins whatever its action.","Incorrect. Firewalls generally accept overlapping rules without complaint. Some have shadowed-rule analysis tools, but the rule is installed and simply never reached."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A new appliance is wired between the core switch and the distribution switch so that all traffic must pass through it and it can drop packets. Which deployment attribute describes it?",c:["Passive deployment via a network TAP","Passive deployment via a SPAN port","Active deployment, inline on the link","Out-of-band management network"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A TAP passively copies traffic to a sensor, so the device would only observe and never block.","Incorrect. A SPAN port mirrors traffic to a monitoring device. It also provides copies, not control of live traffic.","Correct. An active, inline device sits in the traffic path and can inspect and act on packets, including blocking.","Incorrect. Out-of-band management refers to a separate network used to administer devices, not to a device in the production traffic path."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"An enterprise Wi-Fi network uses 802.1X. The access points must send user credentials and accounting data to a central server over UDP. Which AAA protocol is MOST likely used?",c:["TACACS+ (TCP 49) for device administration","LDAPS (TCP 636) for directory queries","Kerberos (port 88) for domain ticketing","RADIUS (UDP 1812/1813) for network login"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. TACACS+ uses TCP and is typically used for administrative logins to network devices. It would be the answer for per-command authorization on routers.","Incorrect. LDAPS queries a directory over TCP with TLS. The RADIUS server may use it behind the scenes, but it is not what the AP speaks.","Incorrect. Kerberos issues tickets for domain authentication to services. It is not the protocol access points use to talk to an authentication server.","Correct. RADIUS is the standard AAA protocol for network access such as Wi-Fi, VPN and 802.1X, and it uses UDP for authentication and accounting."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Network engineers need AAA for logins to routers and switches, with each command authorized and logged separately and the entire payload encrypted. Which protocol is BEST?",c:["RADIUS (UDP 1812/1813) for network login","TACACS+ (TCP 49) for admin access","Kerberos (port 88) for domain ticketing","SAML 2.0 over HTTPS for web SSO"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. RADIUS combines authentication with authorization and encrypts only the password. It is better for network access such as Wi-Fi and VPN.","Correct. TACACS+ separates authentication, authorization and accounting, supports per-command authorization and encrypts the whole payload, which suits device administration.","Incorrect. Kerberos provides ticket-based authentication within a domain and does not authorize individual commands on network devices. It would fit single sign-on to domain services.","Incorrect. SAML federates web single sign-on between identity providers and applications. It does not handle command authorization on routers."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"An architect is deciding where on the network a security appliance should sit so that it can see the traffic it is meant to inspect. Which infrastructure consideration is this?",c:["Fail mode when the device breaks","Latency added to inspected traffic","Placement within the network topology","Licensing model and support cost"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Fail mode defines how the device behaves when it breaks, either open or closed. It does not decide what traffic it sees.","Incorrect. Latency impact concerns delay added to traffic, mostly for inline devices. It would be considered after placement is decided.","Correct. Placement, whether at the perimeter, in the DMZ, at the core or on a host, determines what traffic a device can inspect.","Incorrect. Licensing affects cost, features and support, but it does not determine which traffic a device can see. It would matter when choosing between product tiers."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"An administrator needs secure command-line administration of Linux servers over the network. Which option should be used?",c:["SSH with key-based authentication (TCP 22)","RDP with network level authentication (TCP 3389)","SFTP file transfer over SSH (TCP 22)","TLS-protected web management console (TCP 443)"],a:0,e:["Correct. SSH encrypts the session and offers a remote command shell, and key-based authentication strengthens login security.","Incorrect. RDP provides a remote graphical desktop, mainly for Windows hosts. It would fit administering Windows servers, not Linux shells.","Incorrect. SFTP uses the same port and encryption but is limited to file transfers, so it cannot give an interactive command line.","Incorrect. A web console provides a browser-based interface to an application or device. It does not provide a general command-line shell on the server."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Which statement correctly compares a layer 4 firewall with a layer 7 firewall?",c:["Layer 4 filters on IP, port and state but cannot tell apart applications on one port","Layer 4 inspects URLs and application payloads, so it can block SQL injection in web requests","Layer 4 decrypts TLS sessions in order to identify the applications running inside them","Layer 4 evaluates user identity and application category in each rule of the rule base"],a:0,e:["Correct. A layer 4 firewall sees only transport-level information, so it cannot tell applications apart if they use the same port. Layer 7 firewalls understand application content.","Incorrect. Payload and URL inspection are layer 7 capabilities, typically in a WAF or next-generation firewall.","Incorrect. TLS decryption is a feature of application-aware or proxy-based devices. A layer 4 firewall passes the encrypted stream without opening it.","Incorrect. Rules based on user identity and application category are features of next-generation firewalls. Layer 4 policy relies on addresses, ports and state."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A company places one firewall between the internet and its DMZ and a second firewall between the DMZ and the internal LAN, in series. What does this design achieve?",c:["High availability through automatic failover between the two firewalls","Load balancing of inbound traffic across both firewalls in parallel","Layered defense, with two barriers to breach before reaching the LAN","Air-gap isolation of the internal LAN from every other network"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Failover requires two firewalls in the same position as a redundant pair. Here they sit in series on different boundaries, so one failing does not replace the other.","Incorrect. Load balancing spreads traffic across parallel devices, but this traffic passes through both firewalls in sequence.","Correct. Dual firewalls with a screened subnet between them provide defense in depth, forcing an attacker through two separate control points.","Incorrect. The internal LAN remains connected through the second firewall, so it is not air-gapped. An air gap would have no network path at all, which this design clearly does."]},

// ---------- 3.3 Protecting data (22) ----------
{o:"3.3",q:"A customer record is loaded into RAM while an application calculates an invoice. Which data state does this describe?",c:["Data at rest","Data in use","Data in transit","Data in backup"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Data at rest is stored on persistent media such as disks or databases. This record is currently being processed in memory.","Correct. Data being actively processed in memory or by the CPU is in use, which is why it needs protections such as secure enclaves.","Incorrect. Data in transit is moving across a network. Nothing in the stem says the record is being sent anywhere.","Incorrect. Data in backup is a stored copy kept for recovery. The record is live and under active processing."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A company needs to protect sensitive data while the application is actively processing it in memory. Which technology is DESIGNED for this?",c:["TLS 1.3 session encryption with forward secrecy","Full-disk encryption with a TPM-sealed key","Tokenization of stored values in a vault","Confidential computing in a secure enclave"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. TLS protects data moving across a network and ends at the endpoint, where the data is decrypted. It is the control for data in transit.","Incorrect. Full-disk encryption protects stored data when the device is powered off or stolen. The data is decrypted once loaded into memory, so it is a data at rest control.","Incorrect. Tokenization substitutes stored values with tokens and reduces exposure at rest. The application still needs the real value when it processes it.","Correct. Secure enclaves and confidential computing isolate and encrypt data during processing, protecting data in use even from the host operating system."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A call-center screen displays a customer's Social Security number as ***-**-6789. Which technique is being used?",c:["Tokenization with a mapping vault","Salted hashing of the stored value","Partial data masking of the field value","Format-preserving encryption of the field"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Tokenization replaces the entire value with a surrogate and stores the original in a vault. It would not leave the last four digits visible.","Incorrect. A hash would produce an unreadable fixed-length digest. It would not show the last four digits in their original form.","Correct. Masking hides part of a value while keeping its format, so staff can verify a customer without seeing the whole number.","Incorrect. Format-preserving encryption turns the whole number into a different number of the same format. It would not leave the last four digits visible."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A payment processor replaces card numbers with random surrogate values and keeps the real numbers only in a secured vault that maps tokens back. Which technique is this?",c:["Tokenization of the full card number","Data masking of the card number","Salted hashing of the card number","Obfuscation by character substitution"],a:0,e:["Correct. Tokenization swaps real values for tokens that have no mathematical relationship to them, with a vault holding the mapping. It reduces PCI DSS scope.","Incorrect. Masking hides some characters of a value but usually has no vault. It would fit partially displayed card numbers on a receipt.","Incorrect. Hashing is one-way, so the original cannot be retrieved from a vault. It fits password storage and integrity checks.","Incorrect. Character substitution scrambles data without a controlled lookup. It is weaker, because the pattern may be reversible, and it has no vault mapping."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"An application stores user passwords so that even administrators cannot recover the originals but the system can still verify a login attempt. Which method should it use?",c:["Symmetric encryption with AES-256","Tokenization with a mapping vault","Base64 encoding of each password","Salted hashing of every password"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. AES encryption is reversible by anyone who has the key, so administrators could recover the passwords. It fits data that must be read again.","Incorrect. Tokenization is reversible through the vault, so the original passwords would remain retrievable. It fits payment data that must be restored for processing.","Incorrect. Base64 is an encoding rather than a protection, and anyone can decode it instantly without a key. It would only be used to make binary data safe for text channels.","Correct. A salted hash is a one-way function: the system hashes what the user types and compares results, and no one can reverse the stored value."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A company publishes regulatory filings on its website after redacting all personal details. Which classification do the published versions MOST likely have?",c:["Confidential data classification","Public information classification","Restricted data classification","Sensitive data classification"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Confidential data is meant for limited internal use and could harm the company if disclosed. The filings were deliberately released.","Correct. After redaction and intentional release, the documents are meant for anyone to read and carry the public classification.","Incorrect. Restricted is the highest internal sensitivity tier. It would be applied to unreleased material such as M&A plans.","Incorrect. Sensitive data requires protection against unauthorized disclosure. The redacted filings no longer need that protection."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A hospital stores patient health records that are subject to legally mandated protections and breach notification rules. Which data type is this?",c:["Trade secret business data","Regulated compliance and privacy data","Patented intellectual property","Legal case and contract data"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A trade secret derives value from being kept confidential and is protected by the company's own measures, not by health regulation.","Correct. Regulated data, such as PHI under HIPAA, carries legal handling requirements and penalties for exposure.","Incorrect. Patented intellectual property is protected by the patent system and made public through filing. Patient records are not an invention.","Incorrect. Legal data refers to contracts, filings and records related to legal matters. It does not describe patient information subject to HIPAA."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A beverage company's secret formula is protected only by keeping it confidential inside the company. How is this data BEST classified?",c:["Regulated personal or financial records","Legal case and contract data","Public marketing information","Trade secret proprietary information"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Regulated data is controlled by external laws such as HIPAA or PCI DSS. A formula is protected through the company's own secrecy measures.","Incorrect. Legal data relates to contracts, filings and records of legal matters, not to proprietary recipes. It would fit a question about case files or litigation holds.","Incorrect. Public data is intended for open release, and disclosing the formula would destroy its value. It would fit marketing material that the company wants everyone to see.","Correct. A trade secret has value because it is secret, and protection depends on the owner keeping it confidential rather than on registration."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"GDPR requires that personal data about EU residents be handled under EU law even when it is stored in another country. Which concept is this?",c:["Data sovereignty under applicable law","Data residency of the storage location","Data retention period for records","Data classification by sensitivity"],a:0,e:["Correct. Data sovereignty means data is subject to the laws of a jurisdiction, here following the data subjects wherever the data is stored.","Incorrect. Data residency refers to the physical location where data is stored. The stem is about which law applies regardless of location.","Incorrect. Data retention defines how long data is kept before disposal. It does not decide which jurisdiction's laws apply.","Incorrect. Data classification labels data by sensitivity to drive handling rules. It does not determine legal jurisdiction."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A company configures its cloud account so customer databases can be created only in EU data centers. What is this control?",c:["Permission restriction on user accounts","Network segmentation of database servers","Geographic restriction on regions","Data masking of sensitive fields"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Permission restrictions control who can access data using ACLs and roles. They would not constrain the region where the database is created.","Incorrect. Segmentation isolates systems into network zones and does not decide in which country data resides.","Correct. Geographic restrictions limit where data may be stored or accessed, often to satisfy sovereignty and residency rules.","Incorrect. Masking hides values on display or in test copies. It has no effect on where data is stored."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which method BEST protects the confidentiality of data traveling between a browser and a web server?",c:["Full-disk encryption with BitLocker","Data masking in the web application","Database column encryption","TLS 1.3 encryption of the session"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. BitLocker protects data at rest on a drive. The data is decrypted as it is read and sent, so it provides no protection on the wire.","Incorrect. Masking limits what is displayed to a user but does not encrypt traffic. The unmasked data could still be intercepted in transit.","Incorrect. Column encryption protects stored values at rest in a database. It is valuable, but the question concerns data moving across a network.","Correct. TLS encrypts the session between client and server, protecting data in transit against eavesdropping and tampering."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which statement BEST distinguishes the 'restricted' classification from 'confidential' in a common corporate scheme?",c:["Confidential is usually the top tier with the tightest access, while restricted sits one level below it","Restricted is usually the top tier with the tightest access, while confidential sits right below it","Restricted data may be shared with any employee, while confidential data needs approval before sharing","Confidential data is released to partners by default, while restricted data stays inside the company"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. This reverses the order. Restricted data is the one with the tightest controls in most corporate schemes.","Correct. In many schemes restricted sits at the top, for material such as M&A plans, with confidential just beneath it for sensitive but broader business data.","Incorrect. Restricted data is limited to a very small group, and it is not shared with all employees.","Incorrect. Neither tier is shared by default. Release to partners usually requires an NDA and approval, and it falls under a lower classification."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"An analyst needs production-like data to test a new application but must not see real customer details. Which approach keeps the data realistic yet safe?",c:["Grant read-only access to a replica of the production database","Provide an encrypted copy of production data along with its key","Use a masked copy of the production data","Restore a full production backup into a segmented test network"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A read-only replica prevents changes but still shows the real data to the analyst. It addresses integrity, not confidentiality.","Incorrect. Giving the key along with the copy defeats the encryption, since the analyst can read everything. It would be fine for a trusted system administrator.","Correct. Masking or obfuscating the copy preserves the structure and realism that testing needs without exposing real customer values.","Incorrect. Network segmentation limits access to the environment but the data inside remains real. Analysts could still see customer details."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A company needs data to be unreadable to anyone without a key, while still allowing authorized users to restore the original content. Which technique fits?",c:["AES-256 encryption with a managed key","SHA-256 cryptographic hashing","Static data masking of the field","Vault-based tokenization of values"],a:0,e:["Correct. Encryption provides reversible confidentiality: only holders of the key can decrypt the data back to its original form.","Incorrect. Hashing is one-way and cannot restore the original data. It fits integrity checks and password storage.","Incorrect. Masking hides parts of a value but is not key-based, and the original may not be recoverable from the masked version.","Incorrect. Tokenization is reversible through a lookup vault, not through a cryptographic key. It replaces values rather than encrypting them."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A company grants only the finance group read access to the budget folder. Which data protection method is this?",c:["Permission restrictions via ACLs","Geographic restrictions on regions","Tokenization of folder contents","Salted hashing of the documents"],a:0,e:["Correct. Permission restrictions use ACLs and roles to control which identities can read or modify data.","Incorrect. Geographic restrictions control where data can be stored or accessed from. The stem is about who has access.","Incorrect. Tokenization replaces sensitive values with surrogates. The budget files still contain real values for authorized users.","Incorrect. Hashing is a one-way transformation, so it cannot provide authorized read access to the folder contents."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which choice BEST describes segmentation used as a data protection method?",c:["Encrypting each file with a unique key so that one stolen key exposes only that file","Replacing displayed values with partial characters so viewers see less of each record","Keeping sensitive data in its own network zone so a breach elsewhere cannot reach it","Wrapping all client sessions in TLS so that intercepted packets cannot be read"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. This describes granular encryption with per-file keys. It limits key compromise impact but does not isolate the data on the network.","Incorrect. This describes masking, which limits what viewers see on a screen. It does not isolate where the data is stored.","Correct. Placing sensitive data in its own zone, behind controls, limits what attackers who compromise other systems can reach.","Incorrect. This describes transport encryption such as TLS. It protects data in transit but doesn't separate storage or access paths."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which data type is BEST described as data that, if lost or unavailable, would stop core business operations?",c:["Restricted data classification","Critical data classification level","Regulated data classification","Proprietary data classification"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Restricted is a sensitivity tier that governs who may see the data. Highly restricted data is not necessarily operationally essential.","Correct. Critical data is essential to operations, so its loss or unavailability halts the business. It drives backup and recovery priorities.","Incorrect. Regulated data is governed by external laws. It may or may not be operationally critical.","Incorrect. Proprietary data belongs to the company and gives it competitive advantage. Losing it does not necessarily stop operations."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which pair correctly matches data types?",c:["Human-readable: a compiled binary program; non-human-readable: a plain-text report","Human-readable: encrypted ciphertext on disk; non-human-readable: an HTML web page","Human-readable: a barcode image on a label; non-human-readable: a printed contract","Human-readable: a plain-text report; non-human-readable: a compiled binary file"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. This pairing is backwards. A compiled binary cannot be read without tools, while plain text can.","Incorrect. Ciphertext is unreadable to people, and an HTML page is readable text. The categories are swapped.","Incorrect. A barcode needs a scanner to be interpreted, while a printed contract can be read directly. The categories are reversed.","Correct. Human-readable data can be understood directly, while non-human-readable data such as binaries, encoded files or barcodes needs software to interpret."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A company digitally watermarks confidential spreadsheets and also encrypts them. What does the watermark ADD?",c:["Confidentiality of contents from unauthorized readers","Availability of the files during outages","Traceability of leaked copies to their source","Anonymity of the original author"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Confidentiality comes from the encryption. A watermark is visible or embedded and does not stop anyone from reading a decrypted copy.","Incorrect. A watermark does nothing for uptime or recovery. Availability is addressed with redundancy and backups.","Correct. Watermarks embed identifying marks that help trace a leak back to its source and deter misuse, while encryption handles confidentiality.","Incorrect. A watermark identifies a document's origin or recipient, which reduces anonymity rather than providing it."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A startup processes EU customers' data on US servers. Which concept MOST directly creates legal obligations over where and how that data is handled?",c:["Data sovereignty and jurisdiction","Data classification labeling policy","Data retention scheduling policy","Data minimization practice policy"],a:0,e:["Correct. Sovereignty and jurisdiction rules, such as GDPR, follow the data subject's rights and impose obligations on handling, transfer and storage location.","Incorrect. Classification labeling assigns sensitivity levels to guide handling. It is an internal practice and does not by itself create legal obligations.","Incorrect. Retention scheduling decides how long data is kept. It is shaped by law but does not govern where data may be processed.","Incorrect. Minimization limits the data collected to what is needed. It is a GDPR principle but not the concept about location and jurisdiction."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"What is the PRIMARY purpose of data classification?",c:["Schedule disposal of aged records on a retention timeline","Tier storage by access frequency to lower hosting costs","Define which departments own each data set and its use","Apply controls proportional to sensitivity and value"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Disposal timing is decided by retention policy. Classification may influence it, but it is not the purpose.","Incorrect. Storage tiering is a cost and performance decision based on access patterns, not a security classification.","Incorrect. Assigning ownership is a governance step, with owners then deciding the classification. It is not the purpose of classification itself.","Correct. Classification labels data so that access, encryption, handling and sharing rules match its sensitivity and value."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which option is an example of obfuscation?",c:["Computing a SHA-256 checksum of a download to verify integrity","Replacing customer names with pseudonyms in an analytics set","Encrypting a database with AES and sharing the key with analysts","Rotating encryption keys quarterly to limit exposure of any one key"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A checksum verifies integrity and does not hide anything about the data. It is an integrity control.","Correct. Obfuscation, including pseudonymization, makes data hard to link to real individuals while keeping it usable for analysis.","Incorrect. Encrypting and then sharing the key exposes the data in full to those who receive the key. It does not obscure the data from them.","Incorrect. Key rotation limits the exposure of any single key. It does not transform the data to make it harder to interpret."]},

// ---------- 3.4 Resilience and recovery (24) ----------
{o:"3.4",q:"A company takes a full backup every Sunday. It wants the daily backups to use minimal network bandwidth and storage and accepts slower restores. Which daily backup type fits BEST?",c:["Differential backups each night","Incremental backups each night","Full system backups each night","Mirrored copies each night"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Differentials copy everything changed since the last full, so they grow each day and use more storage and bandwidth. They restore faster, needing only the full plus the latest differential.","Correct. Incrementals copy only changes since the last backup of any type, so they are the smallest and fastest to run. A restore needs the full backup plus every incremental since.","Incorrect. Daily full backups are the heaviest on bandwidth and storage. They offer the simplest and fastest restores.","Incorrect. A mirror continuously replicates the current state, which uses bandwidth constantly and also copies deletions or corruption. It is not a historical backup."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A company runs a full backup on Sunday and differential backups Monday through Friday. The server fails Thursday morning before that day's backup runs. Which backups are needed to restore?",c:["Sunday full plus the Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday differentials","Sunday full plus the Tuesday differential","Wednesday differential alone","Sunday full plus the Wednesday differential"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Applying every day's backup is how incrementals work. Each differential already includes the earlier days' changes.","Incorrect. The Tuesday differential is missing Wednesday's changes. The latest differential before the failure is needed.","Incorrect. A differential contains only changes since the full. Without the Sunday full as a base, the data is incomplete.","Correct. Each differential holds all changes since the last full, so the restore needs the full plus only the most recent differential, which is Wednesday's."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A development team needs to return a virtual machine to exactly how it was before a failed update an hour ago, as fast as possible. Which option is BEST?",c:["Revert to a hypervisor snapshot","Restore from a tape backup","Recover from an offsite cold storage copy","Rebuild from last night's differential backup"],a:0,e:["Correct. A snapshot captures point-in-time VM state, so reverting takes moments and returns to the exact state before the update.","Incorrect. Tape restores are slow because media must be retrieved and read sequentially. Tape suits long-term retention.","Incorrect. Offsite cold storage copies take time to retrieve and are used for disasters, not quick rollbacks.","Incorrect. Last night's differential would also discard the work done since then, and the restore is slower than a revert. It does not return to the state an hour ago."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Developers need to restore a build server to any point in time during the last several hours, not just to last night's state. Which approach supports this BEST?",c:["Nightly differential backups","Weekly full backups to disk","Continuous data protection (CDP)","Monthly tape rotation offsite"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Nightly differentials give one restore point per day, so recovery would return to last night. They are cheaper but coarse.","Incorrect. Weekly fulls provide only weekly restore points. They suit long-cycle retention rather than fine-grained rollback.","Correct. Continuous data protection records every change as it happens, so data can be restored to almost any moment within the retention window.","Incorrect. Monthly tape rotation provides monthly restore points for archival. It cannot restore state from a few hours ago."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"An organization keeps a second data center with live, synchronized systems and can switch over within minutes. Which type of site is this?",c:["Warm site (partially equipped facility)","Cold site (empty shell facility)","Mobile site (trailer-based facility)","Hot site (fully equipped facility)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A warm site has equipment but needs data and configuration loaded, so recovery takes hours to days. It is a cost compromise.","Incorrect. A cold site provides space and utilities only, so recovery takes days to weeks. It is the cheapest.","Incorrect. A mobile site is a portable facility such as a trailer brought on site. It is not a synchronized second data center.","Correct. A hot site is fully equipped and kept current, so failover is fast. It is the most expensive option."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A backup facility has power, cooling and network connections but no servers installed. Which type of site is this?",c:["Warm site (partial equipment)","Cold site (bare shell facility)","Hot site (live mirrored systems)","Mobile site (trailer-based)"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A warm site already has hardware installed and often connectivity. Equipment is the difference from this facility.","Correct. A cold site is space and utilities only. It costs the least and takes the longest to bring into service.","Incorrect. A hot site is fully equipped and running with current data, so it can take over in minutes. This facility has no servers installed, which rules it out.","Incorrect. A mobile site arrives with its own equipment in a trailer or container. This facility is a fixed building without servers."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A company places its backup data center 500 miles away, on a different power grid and outside the primary site's hurricane zone. Which principle is this?",c:["Failover clustering of server nodes","Load balancing of client requests","Geographic dispersion of the sites","Capacity planning for user growth"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Clustering links servers for failover and is typically within a site or a close region. It does not protect against regional disasters by itself.","Incorrect. Load balancing distributes requests across servers to improve performance and availability. Nothing in the stem concerns request distribution, only the location of the site.","Correct. Geographic dispersion places sites far apart, on separate grids and outside the same hazard zone, so a regional disaster cannot take out both the primary and the backup.","Incorrect. Capacity planning forecasts future demand for resources. It does not decide where to locate sites."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A database is copied in near real time to a second server in another city so that it is ready to take over. Which technique is this?",c:["Cross-site data replication","Transaction log journaling","Scheduled point-in-time snapshots","Offsite tape vaulting rotation"],a:0,e:["Correct. Replication continuously copies data to another system or location, keeping a current duplicate that can take over quickly if the primary site fails.","Incorrect. Journaling logs changes locally so a file system or database can recover to a consistent state after a crash. It does not copy data to another city.","Incorrect. Snapshots capture state at a moment in time and are normally stored with the original system. They are not a continuous copy.","Incorrect. Tape vaulting moves tape backups to a secure offsite location on a schedule. It is slower and not near real time."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A file system records each change in a log before applying it, so after a crash it can replay or roll back to a consistent state. Which technique is this?",c:["Metadata and data journaling of changes","Remote data replication to a site","RAID 1 disk mirroring of volumes","Scheduled point-in-time snapshots"],a:0,e:["Correct. Journaling keeps a transaction log of intended changes, supporting recovery and consistency after unexpected failures.","Incorrect. Replication copies data to another system. It does not log operations for replay on the same volume.","Incorrect. Disk mirroring writes identical data to two disks for redundancy, so corruption is copied to both. It does not provide a replay log.","Incorrect. Snapshots capture the state at a moment and do not record the stream of individual changes."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Which power component provides immediate battery power for several minutes so servers can shut down gracefully or ride out brief outages?",c:["Diesel generator with a fuel tank","Managed rack power distribution unit (PDU)","Uninterruptible power supply (UPS)","Surge protector with line filtering"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A generator supplies long-term power but takes time to start, so it needs a UPS to cover the gap.","Incorrect. A PDU distributes power to equipment within a rack and may monitor it, but it does not store energy. Without a battery it cannot bridge an outage.","Correct. A UPS switches to battery instantly when utility power drops, giving minutes of runtime to bridge a gap or shut down cleanly.","Incorrect. A surge protector suppresses voltage spikes to protect equipment. It provides no power during an outage and would fit a question about transient overvoltage."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A data center must remain powered for several days during a regional outage. What is required in addition to a UPS?",c:["Second managed PDU in each rack","Generator with an onsite fuel supply","Larger UPS battery bank in each row","Dual feeds from the same utility substation"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Another PDU adds distribution redundancy within the rack, but it only passes along power it receives. It does not generate any power during a long outage.","Correct. Generators provide extended power, while the UPS carries the load during the seconds it takes them to start.","Incorrect. Larger batteries extend runtime to minutes or hours, not days, and they take up considerable space and cost.","Incorrect. Two feeds from the same substation protect against a cable failure, but a regional outage takes down both."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Three servers share one virtual IP address, exchange heartbeats and present themselves as a single system. If one fails, another takes over its workload. What is this?",c:["Load-balanced pool of web servers","Replicated database with a standby","RAID 5 array with a hot spare","Active/passive failover cluster"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A load-balanced pool spreads requests across independent servers through a separate balancer. The nodes do not coordinate as one system.","Incorrect. Replication copies data between systems but does not by itself make servers take over each other's workloads.","Incorrect. RAID 5 protects data against a single disk failure within one array. It operates at the disk level, not across servers.","Correct. A failover cluster presents multiple nodes as one service and shifts workload to survivors when a node fails, as detected by the heartbeat."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A company uses firewalls from two different vendors in a layered design so that a single vendor vulnerability does not compromise both. Which principle is this?",c:["Geographic dispersion of locations","Failover clustering of nodes","Platform diversity in the stack","Capacity planning for demand"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Geographic dispersion separates sites by distance to survive regional disasters. The stem is about using different vendors, not about where the equipment is located.","Incorrect. Clustering links similar nodes so one can take over for another. Identical nodes share the same vulnerabilities, so one vendor flaw would still hit them all.","Correct. Platform diversity uses different vendors or technologies so that a single flaw or common-mode failure cannot compromise both layers of the design at once.","Incorrect. Capacity planning forecasts the resources needed for future growth. It does nothing to mitigate a vulnerability that both firewalls from the same vendor would share."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Recovery staff sit around a conference table and talk through their response to a simulated flood, discussing roles and decisions without touching any systems. Which exercise is this?",c:["Tabletop walk-through exercise","Hands-on simulation exercise","Parallel processing recovery test","Full interruption recovery test"],a:0,e:["Correct. A tabletop is a discussion-based walk-through of the plan. It is low cost and low risk.","Incorrect. A simulation involves hands-on execution of response and recovery steps in a controlled setting, which goes beyond discussion.","Incorrect. Parallel processing runs the recovery site alongside production to compare results, which requires working with live systems.","Incorrect. A full interruption test shuts down the primary and moves operations to the recovery site. It is the most disruptive."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"During a test, the recovery site processes live transactions alongside the primary site to confirm the results match, without shutting down the primary. Which test is this?",c:["Tabletop planning exercise","Full interruption failover test","Hands-on simulation exercise","Parallel processing test run"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A tabletop is a discussion-only exercise and does not process any transactions. It would be the right choice for a low-cost review of roles and decisions.","Incorrect. A full interruption test shuts down the primary and switches all operations. Here the primary stays running.","Incorrect. A simulation practices a scenario hands-on, typically with test data, and does not run production workload in parallel.","Correct. Parallel processing runs both sites at once and compares outputs, proving readiness without an outage."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Staff respond to a realistic ransomware scenario in an isolated lab, performing the recovery steps hands-on without touching production. Which type of exercise is this?",c:["Tabletop planning exercise","Hands-on simulation exercise","Parallel processing test run","Full interruption failover test"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A tabletop is limited to discussion around a table. These staff are performing actual recovery steps hands-on, which is the defining feature of a simulation.","Correct. A simulation walks teams through a realistic scenario with hands-on actions in a controlled setting, exercising skills without risking production.","Incorrect. Parallel processing involves running the recovery site with live transactions next to production, not a lab.","Incorrect. A full interruption test switches real operations to the recovery site and disrupts production. The stem says the exercise runs in an isolated lab without touching production."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Leadership forecasts that user growth will exceed server and staffing capacity next year and plans purchases and hiring accordingly. Which activity is this?",c:["Tabletop planning exercise","Capacity planning for growth","Failover testing of systems","Platform diversity of vendors"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A tabletop exercise rehearses incident or disaster response through discussion. It does not forecast future resource or staffing needs, which is the aim of this activity.","Correct. Capacity planning projects future demand across people, technology and infrastructure and arranges resources in advance.","Incorrect. Failover testing verifies that redundant systems take over when a primary fails. It does not plan purchases or hiring for growth, so it fits a resilience question instead.","Incorrect. Platform diversity mixes vendors to avoid common failures. It does not address headcount or growth."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Which backup practice BEST protects against ransomware that encrypts every backup reachable from the network?",c:["Daily differentials written to a mapped network drive","Replication of backups to a second server in the same domain","Daily snapshots stored on the same storage array","Offline or immutable backup copies"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A mapped drive is accessible to the malware, which can encrypt the backups along with the primary data.","Incorrect. A server in the same domain is reachable with the same stolen credentials, so the replica can be encrypted too. It is useful for availability but not for ransomware protection.","Incorrect. Snapshots on the same array are exposed to the same compromise or hardware failure. They give quick rollback but not isolation.","Correct. Offline or immutable copies cannot be modified by malware on the network, so a clean copy survives. This is the intent of the 3-2-1 rule's offline copy."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"What should a company do on a regular schedule to confirm its backups are actually usable?",c:["Run test restores of sample data","Increase backup frequency to hourly","Add a second layer of encryption","Extend retention to seven years"],a:0,e:["Correct. A backup that has never been restored is unproven. Test restores verify data integrity, completeness and the recovery procedure.","Incorrect. Frequency determines how much data could be lost (RPO). More frequent backups that fail to restore are still worthless.","Incorrect. Extra encryption protects confidentiality and makes recovery harder. It doesn't show that restores work.","Incorrect. Retention length is governed by policy and compliance. It says nothing about the integrity of the data."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A security manager asks why backups should be encrypted. What is the BEST reason?",c:["It prevents ransomware from encrypting backup files reachable on the network","It shortens restore times by compressing the stored backup data","It protects confidentiality if backup media is lost or stolen","It removes the need for offsite storage of the backup media"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Encrypted files can still be encrypted again by ransomware, since it works on them as it would any other files. Immutable or offline copies address that threat.","Incorrect. Encryption adds processing overhead and usually slows restores slightly. Compression is a separate feature.","Correct. Backups contain everything valuable, so lost or stolen tapes and disks must be unreadable to whoever finds them.","Incorrect. Offsite copies are still needed to survive site-wide disasters. Encryption protects confidentiality, not survivability."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Which statement describes the trade-off between onsite and offsite backups?",c:["Offsite copies restore much faster, while onsite copies survive site-wide disasters","Onsite and offsite differ only in cost, not in speed or survivability","Offsite copies are exposed to facility fires, while onsite copies are not","Onsite copies restore faster, while offsite copies survive site-wide disasters"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. This reverses the trade-off. Offsite copies must be retrieved or transferred, so restores are slower, and onsite copies are lost in a site disaster.","Incorrect. They differ in restore speed and survivability, as well as cost. Those differences are why both are used.","Incorrect. A fire at the primary facility destroys onsite copies, while offsite copies are at a separate location.","Correct. Local copies give fast restores, and remote copies survive fire, flood or other events that destroy the primary site. Mature plans keep both."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Which recovery test actually shuts down the primary site and moves all operations to the recovery site?",c:["Tabletop discussion exercise","Full interruption cutover test","Structured walkthrough checklist review","Parallel processing test run"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A tabletop is a discussion of the plan and does not affect any systems. It is the least disruptive test, whereas the stem describes the most disruptive one.","Correct. A full interruption test is the most realistic and the most disruptive, since production actually runs from the recovery site.","Incorrect. A walkthrough has teams review the plan steps for completeness and accuracy. Nothing is actually switched, so the primary site keeps running throughout.","Incorrect. Parallel processing keeps the primary site running while the recovery site processes the same transactions. The primary is never shut down, unlike in the scenario described."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Which statement BEST describes the difference between high availability and fault tolerance?",c:["Fault tolerance limits downtime with fast failover, while high availability continues with no interruption","High availability depends on backups to restore service, while fault tolerance depends on replication","High availability limits downtime with fast failover, while fault tolerance has no interruption at all","Fault tolerance accepts brief outages during failover, while high availability prevents any outage"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. This reverses the definitions. Fault tolerance is the one that has no interruption.","Incorrect. Neither term depends on backups, since both rely on redundant components running at the time of the failure.","Correct. High availability recovers quickly, possibly with a brief blip, while fault tolerance absorbs a failure with no interruption at all, usually at greater cost.","Incorrect. This also reverses them. Fault tolerance is the design that allows no outage, and high availability may have a short failover delay."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A rack-mounted PDU can be managed remotely and reports power draw for each outlet. What does it primarily help with?",c:["Distributing power and monitoring each outlet, with remote power cycling","Generating backup power for the rack during extended utility outages","Providing battery runtime so servers can shut down gracefully in a power loss","Filtering voltage spikes and sags to protect equipment from surges"],a:0,e:["Correct. Managed PDUs distribute power within a rack and give per-outlet visibility and control, such as rebooting a hung device remotely.","Incorrect. Generating power is the job of a generator. A PDU only distributes power it receives.","Incorrect. Battery runtime comes from a UPS, which stores energy for outages. A managed PDU only distributes and monitors power and has no energy storage of its own.","Incorrect. Surge suppression is done by surge protectors or line conditioners. Some PDUs include basic surge protection, but monitoring is the primary role described here."]}
]);
